press \\( n \\) as \\( n = 18k \\) for some integer \\( k \\).\n\n2. **Ensure the GCD condition:**\n   We need to ensure that \\(\\gcd(18k, 36) = 18\\). Since \\( 18 = 2 \\times 3^2 \\) and \\( 36 = 2^2 \\times 3^2 \\), the GCD of \\( 18k \\) and 36 will be 18 if and only if \\( k \\) is not divisible by 2 (to avoid the GCD being larger than 18).\n\n3. **Find the smallest \\( k \\) such that \\( 18k > 200 \\):**\n   We need \\( 18k > 200 \\). Solving for \\( k \\):\n   \\[\n   k > \\frac{200}{18} \\approx 11.11\n   \\]\n   The smallest integer greater than 11.11 is 12. However, \\( k \\) must not be divisible by 2. The next integer after 11.11 that is not divisible by 2 is 13.\n\n4. **Calculate \\( n \\):**\n   Using \\( k = 13 \\):\n   \\[\n   n = 18 \\times 13 = 234\n   \\]\n\n5. **Verify the GCD:**\n   \\[\n   \\gcd(234, 36) = \\gcd(18 \\times 13, 36)\n   \\]\n   Since 13 is not divisible by 2, the GCD remains 18.\n\nThus, the smallest integer greater than 200 for which the greatest common divisor with 36 is 18 is \\(\\boxed{234}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{234}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:350"}
